<section class="card" id="culture">
<h2>Chamba Cultural Things and Their Uses</h2>
<div class="grid2">
<div><h3>Gang_gbang'ehn</h3><p>A flute used for lera, mumbara and related cultural performances.</p></div>
<div><h3>Nambán</h3><p>A musical instrument used for lera, gindamen and mumbara and associated with guiding Nangbeuleung.</p></div>
<div><h3>Gindamen / Mumbara</h3><p>A ceremonial dance.</p></div>
<div><h3>Nangbeulang</h3><p>A Chamba mask. Names vary by community: Langbadna in Mapeo, Sugu, Yelli and Leko; Namgbeuleung in Chamba Dakka; Vara in Southern Chamba.</p></div>
<div><h3>Gak_gám</h3><p>Horns, often made from wood or bush horn. Their sound can communicate messages such as war or readiness.</p></div>
<div><h3>Kongla_Nyi_Gam</h3><p>Elephant tusk signal associated with the Gang during burial and other important occasions.</p></div>
<div><h3>Teun_gleung</h3><p>A bell worn by children of the Gang, dignitaries and children who have demonstrated importance through circumcision traditions.</p></div>
<div><h3>Barrinn-dú / Tong'ehn-dú</h3><p>Beer pots associated with cleansing the land when twins are born.</p></div>
<div><h3>Dang_dang / Gin_mi</h3><p>Drums used for jenson and wurum.</p></div>
<div><h3>Ginnoó / Bannoó</h3><p>A drum associated with the death of a Gang or dignitary and with dances.</p></div>
<div><h3>Subalen</h3><p>Worn on women's ankles to add rhythm during movement and dance.</p></div>
<div><h3>Lérá</h3><p>A set of flutes and dance. Six members are common in many communities; Yelli and Bali are noted, while Nyonga sets may have five or four members.</p></div>
<div><h3>Dong_dong</h3><p>A drum used for ceremonies such as naming.</p></div>
</div>
